        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_18-1.lnote.oe.23" n="23" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">59</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">cum <hi rend="italic">fügt zu Lond</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">cum added by Lond</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_18-1.lnote.oe.1-3" n="1" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">61</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">capit. <hi rend="italic">T</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">capit. T</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_18-1.lnote.oe.2-2" n="2" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">61</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">nullus <hi rend="italic">Lond</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">nullus Lond</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_18-1.lnote.oe.3" n="3" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">61</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">nur</hi> in <hi rend="italic">Lond; nur</hi> pro <hi rend="italic">R T</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">only in Lond; only pro R T</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_18-1.lnote.oe.7-2" n="7" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">58</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">letztes</hi> d <hi rend="italic">ausradiert</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">last d erased</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_18-1.lnote.de.1" n="1" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">55</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Die §§ 18, 1—3 fehlen Q <hi rend="smallcaps">ii</hi> und vielleicht daher Lambarde, der Br(omton) kannte</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">§§ 18, 1—3 are lacking in Q <hi rend="smallcaps">ii</hi>, and perhaps for that reason in Lambarde, who knew Br(omton)</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_18-1.lnote.de.2" n="2" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">55</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">nicht ‘verheiratete’, wie Q, laut <hi rend="italic">marito,</hi> versteht. <hi rend="italic">Vgl. Gl</hi> Eheschliessung 9—11</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">not ‘married’, as Q, judging by <hi rend="italic">marito</hi>, understands it. <hi rend="italic">Cf. Gl</hi> Eheschliessung 9—11</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_18-1.lnote.de.3" n="3" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">55</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">Ebd.</hi> 5f. 8r</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">Ibid.</hi> 5f. 8r</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_18-1.lnote.de.4" n="4" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">55</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Das Institut des Brautbürgen ist wohl vor 1100 erstorben, da H cor., B, Q die Worte in ‘dem welchem es zukommt’, bzw. ‘dem Ehemanne’ ändern, und zwar an allen drei Stellen. Der Bräutigamsbürge lebt noch in Wif und dem Heiratskontrakt von 1019 Earle <hi rend="italic">Landcha.</hi> 228</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">The institution of the bride’s surety probably died out before 1100, since H cor., B and Q change the words to ‘to him to whom it is due’ and ‘to the husband’ respectively, and indeed in all three places. The bridegroom’s surety still survives in Wif and in the marriage contract of 1019, Earle <hi rend="italic">Landcha.</hi> 228</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_18-1.lnote.de.5" n="5" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">55</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Diesen Zusatz hat Ld umgestellt; <hi rend="italic">s. o.</hi> Af 10⁶</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Ld has transposed this addition; <hi rend="italic">see above</hi> Af 10⁶</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_18-1.lnote.de.6" n="6" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">55</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">godum</hi> ist nicht adj., <hi rend="italic">man</hi> nicht etwa ‘blemish, <hi rend="italic">mān</hi>’. Vielleicht war <hi rend="italic">feogodum</hi> Randglosse, schon im Archetyp, zu <hi rend="italic">cwicæhtum</hi> (Thorpe). Da <hi rend="italic">feoh</hi> in 20 Compositis ‘tote Fahrnis’ bedeutet, so könnte man hier (<hi rend="italic">s. Gl</hi> Zahlung) ‘Edelmetall, Geld, Futter, Waffen’ verstehen, also einen Gegensatz zum vorigen Worte; dann wäre aber <hi rend="italic">and</hi> davor hinzuzudenken</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">godum</hi> is not an adj., nor is <hi rend="italic">man</hi>, say, ‘blemish, <hi rend="italic">mān</hi>’. Perhaps <hi rend="italic">feogodum</hi> was a marginal gloss, already in the archetype, on <hi rend="italic">cwicæhtum</hi> (Thorpe). Since <hi rend="italic">feoh</hi> in 20 compounds means ‘dead chattels’, one could understand it here (see <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> Zahlung) as ‘precious metal, money, fodder, weapons’, i.e. a contrast to the preceding word; but then <hi rend="italic">and</hi> would have to be supplied before it</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_18-1.lnote.de.7" n="7" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">55</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Bessere die Übs.: ‘man gebe in dies [Bussgeld] hinein keinen’</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Correct the translation to: ‘let none be given into this [compensation]’</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_18-1.lnote.de.8" n="8" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">55</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Nicht ‘Mann’. — <hi rend="italic">Vgl. Gl</hi> unfrei 6e gegen Ine 54, 1</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Not ‘man’. — <hi rend="italic">Cf. Gl</hi> unfrei 6e as against Ine 54, 1</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Af_18-1.attnote.fn.12" n="12" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">72</biblScope></bibl><label>beweddodu</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">beweddo</hi>. B. <hi rend="italic">beweddod</hi>. H.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Af_18-1.attnote.fn.13" n="13" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">72</biblScope></bibl><label>byrgean</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">þe hit gebyrige</hi>. H &amp; B.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Af_18-1.attnote.fn.14" n="14" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">72</biblScope></bibl><label>nænigne</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">næningne</hi>. B.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="Af_18-1.attnote.n.1" n="1" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">195</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Quadr. translates <hi rend="italic">LX solid. emendetur marito.</hi></seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="Af_18-1.attnote.n.2" n="2" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">195</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">feogodum</hi> may be taken as a definition (possibly added later) of <hi rend="italic">cwicæhtum</hi>. This is preferable to supposing that <hi rend="italic">and</hi> has been omitted, which Liebermann suggests as another possibility.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="translationFootnote" xml:id="Af_18-1.whinote.n.1" n="1" source="#whitelock"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">412</biblScope></bibl><label>cattle</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">feogodum.</hi> Liebermann suggests that this was originally only a gloss to <hi rend="italic">cwicæhtum</hi> “livestock”, or, alternatively, that it s an antithesis to this and means “valuables (other than livestock)” with a preceding “and” omitted.</seg></note>
